--TEST--
An `Iterator<T>` is a single position living in the object: a `break` leaves it where it stood, a callee typed `Iterator<T>` moves the caller's own, a drained one runs a `foreach` zero times, and two cursors over one list are independent — `rule:iteration/two-interfaces`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Iterator
// `rule:iteration/two-interfaces`: the position a cursor walks lives in the cursor object, not in
// the frame driving it, and `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects` drives an `Iterator<T>` subject
// without asking it for a fresh one the way it asks an `Iterable<T>`. What this
// pins is that nothing on the way copies that position: not leaving a `foreach`
// early, not a parameter, not a second loop over the same cursor.
class Words implements Iterator<string> {
    private array<string> $items;
    private int $at;

    public function constructor(array<string> $items) {
        $this->items = $items;
        $this->at = -1;
    }

    public function advance(): bool {
        $this->at = $this->at + 1;
        return $this->at < Core\Arr::count($this->items) as int;
    }

    public function current(): string {
        return $this->items[$this->at];
    }
}

final class Drive {
    // A cursor reaching a parameter typed `Iterator<string>` is the caller's own
    // cursor, so the two elements taken here are two the caller will not see.
    // The `advance()` guard is the whole liveness answer, and a cursor that is
    // already exhausted answers `false` on the first call.
    public static function takeTwo(Iterator<string> $cursor): string {
        string $out = "";
        int $taken = 0;
        while ($taken < 2) {
            if (!$cursor->advance()) {
                return $out;
            }
            $out = $out . $cursor->current() . ";";
            $taken = $taken + 1;
        }
        return $out;
    }

    public static function rest(Iterator<string> $cursor): string {
        string $out = "";
        foreach ($cursor as string $w) {
            $out = $out . $w . ";";
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

array<string> $source = ["red", "green", "blue", "grey", "pink"];

// A `break` stops the loop calling `advance()` and leaves the cursor standing on
// the element the body last read. Driving it by hand afterwards resumes at the
// element after that one, so nothing is repeated and nothing is lost — unlike
// `rule:iteration/generators`' cursor, which an abandoning `break` ends for good.
var $paint = new Words($source);
string $upToGreen = "";
foreach ($paint as string $w) {
    $upToGreen = $upToGreen . $w . ";";
    if ($w == "green") {
        break;
    }
}
echo $upToGreen, "\n";
echo Drive::takeTwo($paint), "\n";
echo Drive::rest($paint), "\n";

// Single-pass, which is the whole difference from `Iterable<T>`: the cursor is
// used up, so a `foreach` over it runs zero times rather than starting over.
echo "[", Drive::rest($paint), "]\n";
echo "[", Drive::takeTwo($paint), "]\n";

// Two cursors over the same list hold two positions, and draining one leaves the
// other where it was.
var $a = new Words($source);
var $b = new Words($source);
string $firstTwo = Drive::takeTwo($a);
string $nextTwo = Drive::takeTwo($a);
echo $firstTwo, $nextTwo, "|", Drive::takeTwo($b), "\n";
--EXPECT--
red;green;
blue;grey;
pink;
[]
[]
red;green;blue;grey;|red;green;
